WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:05.260
Captain Marvel is one of Marvel Comics' most
aspirational figures, and has inspired a passionate

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:05.940
fanbase.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:10.480
But when a character has been around for this
long, there are bound to be some unknown corners

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.740
to their history - even for longtime fans.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:17.960
It's hard to find a superhero identity that
hasn't been held by multiple people at some

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:18.460
point.

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:22.500
From Batman to Thor, it's just part of the
territory when it comes to shaking up the

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.340
status quo.

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.150
So it's no surprise that Carol Danvers is
far from the first person to bear the mantle

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.180
of Captain Marvel.

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:34.480
The first Captain Marvel was an alien named
Mar-Vell, a member of the Kree species who

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:39.329
was initially sent to Earth to observe it
as a spy, disguising himself as a human scientist

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:40.440
named Walter Lawson.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.180
He eventually took up the identity of a superhero
to protect the very people he'd been sent

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:45.820
to observe.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.720
But Mar-Vell died in 1982, succumbing to cancer
as a part of Marvel's first graphic novel.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:56.200
After the death of the first Captain Marvel,
the name was passed on to Monica Rambeau,

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:01.080
a New Orleans native who developed a variety
of powers after being exposed to extra-dimensional

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:01.580
energy.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:06.280
Despite having little to do with the original
Captain Marvel, she spent some time using

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:10.540
the name as a member of the Avengers, even
serving as the team's leader at one point.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.000
After Monica, the name made its way back to
two of Mar-Vell's descendents, a man named

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.400
Genis-Vell, and then a woman named Phyla-Vell.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.340
All the while, Carol Danvers was working in
the wings as Ms. Marvel, with an origin story

00:01:22.340 --> 00:01:25.080
that tied her directly to Mar-Vell and his
power set.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:30.520
It wasn't until 2012 that she advanced to
take the name Captain Marvel for herself,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.980
putting her in the company of a rich legacy
of heroes.

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:38.480
From as far back as the earliest days of Clark
Kent, it's been tricky for superheroes to

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.300
balance personal relationships with their
professional responsibilities.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.720
Look at Peter Parker, whose life has constantly
gone downhill thanks to the balancing act

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:48.340
he has to play as Spider-Man.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.400
Carol Danvers' situation was even worse.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:56.620
For a period of time, Ms. Marvel wasn't so
much Carol Danvers' secret identity as it

00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:59.540
was a completely separate, independent personality.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.620
After an encounter with a Kree device called
the Psyche-Magnitron fused her human DNA with

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:10.060
the alien Mar-Vell, she developed superpowers
- but at first, she wasn't consciously aware

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:10.560
of them.

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:14.680
When her powers kicked in, Carol blacked out
and Ms. Marvel took over.

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.270
It took some help from the inter-dimensional
being Hecate to get her two identities under

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.720
control, but whatever it took, it was worth
it.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:26.400
It's way easier to be a superhero if you can
remember what you did the next day.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:27.840
"Prove you're not a Skrull."

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.780
"A Skrull cannot do that."

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:36.120
While she was introduced as working on the
same Air Force base as Mar-Vell, Ms. Marvel's

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.459
solo series took her away from the military
and into the slightly less-demanding field

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.800
of professional writing.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:46.970
She quickly lands a position as an editor
under J. Jonah Jameson, the Daily Bugle publisher

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:48.820
who's always giving grief to Peter Parker.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:54.300
Carol is brought on by Jameson as an editor
of a women's interest magazine, the content

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.000
of which the two butted heads over.

00:02:56.590 --> 00:03:01.040
The cigar-chomping Jameson expected fashion
tips and housekeeping advice, while Carol

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:04.320
wanted to push the publication as a progressive
platform for women.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:09.060
The plotline established Carol as a character
with an agenda, and the confidence to pursue

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:09.560
it.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.560
Her championing of women's rights reflected
the era's ideals of empowerment and liberation.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:19.800
Writer Kelly Sue DeConnick, who introduced
Carol as the new Captain Marvel in 2012, said

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.099
of the debut series,

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:25.720
"I'm struck now by how overtly progressive
it was, even by today's standards."

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:31.160
It took decades for Carol to become a Captain,
but she's been a superhero since the late

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:33.560
70s - and not always as Ms. Marvel, either.

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:38.660
Binary, Carol's second superhero identity
came about after an encounter with Rogue,

00:03:38.660 --> 00:03:43.460
a power-sapping member of the X-Men, who temporarily
stripped Carol of her Kree powers.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.450
She was later subject to experimentation by
aliens known as the Brood, the results of

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.280
which left her able to get back into the superhero
action by drawing on the power of a literal

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:53.780
star.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.140
During this period she lost her emotional
ties to Earth, leaving her free to venture

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.860
into outer space for new adventures, often
as an ally of the X-Men.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.960
But by the late 90s, she was angling back
toward her original skillset, losing her connection

00:04:06.970 --> 00:04:11.970
to her cosmic Binary powers and taking on
the alias of Warbird and fighting as a member

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:12.920
of the Avengers.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.980
But the loss of her Binary powers brought
Carol to a personal low.

00:04:17.300 --> 00:04:21.760
At her worst, she developed a drinking habit
and became professionally unreliable, with

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:25.000
her conduct eventually leading to her suspension
from the team.

00:04:25.420 --> 00:04:29.690
Afterwards, she spent a few years as more
of a background character before returning

00:04:29.690 --> 00:04:34.380
to prominence - and her old name - with a
second Ms. Marvel series in 2006.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.220
But as dark as these years were for Carol,
becoming an alcoholic and getting kicked off

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:42.400
the Avengers is far from the worst thing that's
happened to her.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.900
Superhero stories can take their characters
to some weird places, but one plot turn from

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.300
Carol's past was so twisted that it nearly
ruined her as a character.

00:04:52.610 --> 00:04:58.640
It all took place in a single issue, 1980's
Avengers #200, only three years after Carol

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:00.460
had made her debut as Ms. Marvel.

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:04.400
It's now widely considered to be one of the
worst comic books ever written.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.600
In the story, the Avengers discover that Ms.
Marvel has become pregnant under mysterious

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.340
circumstances.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:14.620
That in itself isn't a problem - just ask
Carol's friend Spider-Woman.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.440
The bizarre part is that the entire nine-month
development of her pregnancy takes course

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:25.040
over just a few days, resulting in a child
who ages at an impossibly fast rate.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.080
The baby quickly grows into a man named Marcus,
who explains that he was previously trapped

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.570
in an alternate dimension called Limbo, a
world without time in which he was born.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:38.520
He only escaped Limbo by being "born" again
through Ms. Marvel.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.140
Things turn from bizarre to frightening when
Marcus reveals that he actually kidnapped

00:05:43.150 --> 00:05:47.900
Carol, subjecting her to several weeks' worth
of manipulation and mind control - after which

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.440
he impregnated her with his essence, and erased
her memory of the event.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.960
To call this development creepy is a serious
understatement.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.140
"That's illegal."

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.139
Instead of throwing him into the sun, Carol
is written as developing romantic feelings

00:06:01.139 --> 00:06:05.920
for the interdimensional sex criminal who
is also her son, deciding to leave the Avengers

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.440
to go back to his world with him.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.440
Making things even worse, Carol's teammates
don't see anything wrong with any of it.

00:06:12.880 --> 00:06:16.680
As writer and artist Carol Strickland wrote
in an essay criticizing the issue,

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:20.300
"The entire plot is a deadly insult to every
woman."

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:25.040
Fortunately for Carol's legacy, the bizarre
story was effectively retconned the next year

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:29.240
by writer Chris Claremont, who previously
wrote for the Ms. Marvel solo series.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.080
Claremont took great pains to fix the mess
this story left, making it clear that Carol

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.520
didn't fall in love with her attacker, but
was instead brainwashed.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:42.380
And when she's rescued from Marcus' clutches,
she takes the Avengers to task with righteous

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:46.000
fury for standing by and doing nothing while
she fell into the villain's grasp.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:51.680
It's hard to imagine Netflix's Jessica Jones
without Trish Walker, but Trish wasn't the

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:54.180
first character intended to fill this supporting
role.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:59.240
The original plans for the show included none
other than Carol Danvers as Jessica's best

00:06:59.240 --> 00:06:59.740
friend.

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:04.080
Considering Carol's history of intergalactic
adventures, her inclusion would have made

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:08.100
for quite a different show - or at least a
very stripped-down version of Carol.

00:07:08.690 --> 00:07:12.900
Now that Netflix's Marvel partnership has
come to an end, it's probably for the best

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:15.000
that Carol stayed on the film side of the
equation.

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:19.840
After all, Captain Marvel once fought off
an entire fleet of spaceships single-handedly,

00:07:20.260 --> 00:07:22.780
and that's the kind of thing that begs to
be seen in a movie.

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:26.400
Her powers are big-budget; what did Hawkeye
ever do that compares to that?

